--- a/CONTROL_HELPERS.md
+++ b/CONTROL_HELPERS.md
@@ -26,3 +26,33 @@ Operation-specific encoder and imaging c
 their service implementations. A helper is responsible for serialising
 changes to shared camera resources and for returning success only after a
 change is applied. The server does not commit its generated configuration.
+
+## Encoder operations
+
+`get` receives `tokens`, an array of configured encoder tokens. Return
+`revision` and `profiles`, exactly one full configuration per requested token.
+Each entry contains `token`, `type` (`JPEG`, `H264`, `H265`), integer `width`,
+`height`, `bitrate` (kbps), numeric `frame_rate` (fps) and `quality`.
+Interframe codecs also require `gov_length` and `encoding_profile`.
+`encoding_interval` is optional and defaults to the configured interval.
+These live values replace metadata for the current request only. An
+`unsupported` get retains static metadata; other errors produce SOAP faults.
+
+`options` receives optional `token` and `profile_token` filters. Return
+`revision` and an `options` array. Each codec entry contains `type`,
+`resolutions` (objects with `width` and `height`), `frame_rates` (descending
+positive fps), `quality`, `bitrate`, `encoding_interval` and, for interframe
+codecs, `gov_length` and `encoding_profiles`. Ranges are objects with `min`
+and `max`. Integer ONVIF ranges require integral endpoints. Encoding profiles
+use ONVIF spelling, such as `Baseline`, `Main`, `High`, `Main10`.
+Media1 accepts one entry per codec, in JPEG then H264 order, with the same
+integer quality range. Media2 accepts multiple entries per codec. Options
+must account for shared resources and the requested profile. `unsupported`
+retains the static, current-settings-only options.
+
+`set` receives `token`, `configuration` and `force_persistence`. Configuration
+uses the same fields as get, without token. Return `revision` and the complete
+effective `configuration` after applying it. Media1 persistence is always
+true, as required by the current Media specification; Media2 also persists
+encoder changes. Rejected changes must leave the camera unchanged. Setters
+remain unavailable when no encoder helper is configured.
